If "n" is not 0, the function returns "n" multiplied by the factorial of "n-1". This is where the recursion comes into play. The function calls itself with the argument "n-1" until "n" becomes 0. When "n" becomes 0, the if statement returns 1, and the function starts returning the product of "n" and the previous factorial values.

